{"id":5916,"date":"2019-06-24T21:05:33","date_gmt":"2019-06-25T01:05:33","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/?p=5916"},"modified":"2019-06-24T23:20:27","modified_gmt":"2019-06-25T03:20:27","slug":"volunteering-at-d-c-booth-fish-hatchery","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/2019\/06\/24\/volunteering-at-d-c-booth-fish-hatchery\/","title":{"rendered":"Volunteering at D.C. Booth Fish Hatchery"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<p>Really?  We don&#8217;t fish &#8230; we don&#8217;t cook or even eat ENOUGH fish (according to the health experts) and we don&#8217;t really have any desire to handle fish or even SMELL fish!  Why on earth would we consider working at a fish hatchery as one of our Workamping \/ Volunteering gigs?<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>Our good friends Phil and Cheryl volunteered here in 2016 and other good friends Matt and Sherry volunteered here in 2018 and they ALL highly recommended that we get on the list to volunteer here.  Matt and Sherry were coming back again this year (&#8217;19) and so we submitted our application and resume&#8217;  back in August of 2018 and were accepted as volunteers.  <\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>D.C. Booth Historic National Fish Hatchery and Archives is not your <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">normal <\/span>production fish hatchery.  There are 90 national U.S. Fish &amp; Wildlife fish hatcheries throughout the nation along with many other state-managed hatcheries that are still in full-time operation.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>This hatchery was an active production facility from it&#8217;s completion of construction in 1899 until 1983.  At that time it was closed as a major production facility and turned into the national hatchery education site and archives.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>Volunteering here has given both Kathy and I a new appreciation for serving as &#8220;tour guides&#8221; doing interpretive work.  We were both a little nervous about this new role in our lives as volunteers.  If you&#8217;ve been following this blog for any length of time, you know that most of our volunteer experiences have been working in private or public RV parks or campgrounds as hosts, cleaning crew, or office duties.  Although all of these positions have involved working with the public and have given us the opportunity to meet and talk with a lot of new people, we&#8217;ve not been put in the position of &#8220;tour guide&#8221; doing interpretive work.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>Would we be able to learn the script?  Would we be able to smile all day?  Would we be able to be on our feet for four and a half hours at a time?  We were not concerned about the camp site, the area, or the management.  We were confident that would all be great &#8230; thanks to our friends who&#8217;d worked here before.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>We have to say that as of this writing &#8230; we&#8217;ve only been here about 3 weeks now &#8230; it&#8217;s been a wonderful experience and we&#8217;re quite comfortable with doing the interpretive work (tour guides) and we&#8217;re really enjoying our interactions with the other volunteers and being able to see the many sights that the Black Hills of South Dakota have to offer.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>There are basically four different venues here where the volunteers are scheduled to work.  Our venue assignments are rotated each shift.  Spouses work the same shifts.  We work 3 half-days on duty, then 3 full days off duty.  <\/p>\n\n\n\n<p>We might work in; the Museum, the Fish Car, the Booth House, or the Gift Shop.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"720\" height=\"540\" src=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2245.jpg?fit=685%2C514&amp;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-5918\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2245.jpg 720w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2245-300x225.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2245-768x576.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2245-1024x768.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2245-685x514.jpg 685w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2245-2000x1500.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\" \/><figcaption>The original hatchery building, constructed in 1899<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p>\n\nRemember, you can click on any of the thumbnails below to see an enlarged image\n\n<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-gallery columns-3 is-cropped wp-block-gallery-1 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex\"><li class=\"blocks-gallery-item\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2241-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"\" data-id=\"5932\" data-link=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/?attachment_id=5932\" class=\"wp-image-5932\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2241-1024x768.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2241-300x225.jpg 300w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2241-768x576.jpg 768w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2241-685x514.jpg 685w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2241-2000x1500.jpg 2000w, https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2241.jpg 720w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><figcaption>Looking from the porch to Gift Shop and Pond #3<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/li><li class=\"blocks-gallery-item\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2243-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"\" data-id=\"5933\" data-link=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/?attachment_id=5933\" class=\"wp-image-5933\" srcset=\"https:\/\/herbnkathy.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/06\/IMG_2243-1024x768.